s6-2-p15 = prose (cons "**Finite specialization of an evaluator.** Applying the first projection to a represented evaluator yields a residual with the same represented meaning, but termination of the *specialization algorithm* is an additional question. For an offline specializer that memoizes static configurations, a sufficient condition is that the reachable static configuration shapes form a finite set and that static reduction between memoization points terminates. A fixed source program has finitely many syntactic code positions, but an unbounded continuation stack or recursively varying static argument does **not** thereby have finitely many static states. A binding-time analysis may therefore retain the stack and runtime data dynamically, while using code positions and a finite set of frame constructors as static dispatch information. The residual's control locations then arise from the reachable static shapes; their transitions are computed by specializing the evaluator's cases. More explicitly, let \(Q\subseteq\mathsf{Pos}(c)\times\mathsf{FrameKind}\times\mathsf{StaticEnv}\) be the set of memoized static shapes and \(\delta:Q\to\mathsf{ResidualCase}(Q)\) the partial evaluator's case reduction. If \(Q\) is finite and every call that performs static reduction before returning to a memoization point terminates, then worklist expansion reaches a fixed point after at most \(|Q|\) newly discovered shapes. Each \(q\in Q\) supplies one residual control location; the dynamic continuation stack is a runtime value, not part of \(Q\). The resulting finite control graph has edges obtained from \(\delta(q)\), including dynamic conditional edges and residual calls to the general evaluator when code becomes dynamic. These premises yield a finite residual graph; they do not establish that all evaluator clauses become first-order direct instructions or that the resulting blocks satisfy the structural fold's compositionality without the separate congruence proof. This is a sufficient construction under the stated binding-time premises, not a theorem that every specializer terminates or that every residual has a finite control-flow graph." nil)
